{"thought":"The `ShellComplete` class is responsible for handling shell completions, and it uses the `CompletionItem` class to represent completion suggestions. The issue seems to stem from how the help text is processed and displayed. I will create a new test that checks for the presence of the `--at` option without relying on the help text, and I will also check if the completion system raises any exceptions when the option is invoked. This will help confirm the bug without depending on the help text formatting.","tool":"run_test","args":{"source":"import pytest
import click


def _get_completions(cli, args, incomplete):
    from click.shell_completion import ShellComplete
    comp = ShellComplete(cli, {}, cli.name, "_CLICK_COMPLETE")
    return comp.get_completions(args, incomplete)


def test_multiline_help_option():
    @click.command()
    @click.option(
        "--at",
        type=(str, str),
        multiple=True,
        help="Attachment with explicit mimetype,\n--at image.jpg image/jpeg"
    )
    def cli(at):
        pass

    # Check if the --at option is recognized
    try:
        completions = _get_completions(cli, ["--at"], "")
        assert completions is not None
        assert any(c.value == '--at' for c in completions)
    except Exception as e:
        pytest.fail(f"Completion raised an exception: {e}")"}}
